==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sleydʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|sliden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|slīdan|t=to slide}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*slīdan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*slīdaną|t=to slide, glide}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro||*sléydʰ-e-ti}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*sleydʰ-|t=slippery}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|slītan||to slide}} (whence {{cog|de|schlittern}}), {{cog|gml|slīden||to slide}}, {{cog|dum|slīden||to slide}} (whence {{cog|nl|slijderen}}, frequentative of now obsolete {{m|nl|slijden}}), {{cog|sa-ved|स्रेधति|tr=srédhati||to err, blunder}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/slaɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-slide.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪd|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|slides|sliding|slid|past_ptc2=slidden}}

# {{lb|en|ergative}} To (cause to) move in [[continuous]] [[contact]] with a [[surface]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''slid''' the boat across the grass.}}
#: {{ux|en|The safe '''slid''' slowly.}}
#: {{ux|en|Snow '''slides''' down the side of a mountain.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move on a low-[[friction]] surface.
#: {{ux|en|The car '''slid''' on the ice.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1685|author=w:Edmund Waller|title=Of the Invasion and Defeat of the Turks
|passage=They bathe in summer, and in winter '''slide'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|baseball}} To drop down and [[skid]] into a base.
#: {{ux|en|Jones '''slid''' into second.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To lose one’s [[balance]] on a [[slippery]] surface.
#: {{syn|en|slip}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''slid''' while going around the corner.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To pass or put imperceptibly; to [[slip]].
#: {{ux|en|to alter the meaning of a question by '''sliding''' in a word}}
#: {{ux|en|Schoolchildren sometimes '''slide''' each other notes during class.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1992|month=October|author={{w|Steve Buckley (journalist)|Steve Buckley}}|title=Boss Hog: Jim Lachey is the best offensive lineman in football playing on the best team in football|journal={{w|Sport (US magazine)|Sport}}|volume=83|issue=10|issn=0038-7797|page=64|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sport-magazine-1992-10-c/page/64/mode/2up?q=slide|passage=Lachey and Olson have become rather chummy the last couple of years—they '''slide''' each other free tickets, they visit each other at practice sessions and games, their wives hang out—and, well, Olson has been filling Lachey’s head with a lot of baseball talk.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To subtly direct a facial expression at (someone).
#: {{ux|en|He '''slid''' me a dirty look.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To pass inadvertently.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Ecclesiasticus|28|26|page=1125|column=2|passage=Beware thou '''ſlide''' not by it, leſt thou fall before him that lieth in wait.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To pass along [[smoothly]] or [[unobservedly]]; to move gently onward without [[friction]] or [[hindrance]].
#: {{ux|en|A ship or boat '''slides''' through the water.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1692|author=w:John Dryden|title=Cleomenes, the Spartan Hero, a Tragedy
|passage=Ages shall '''slide''' away without perceiving.}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Works|volume=II|title=Epistle 4|lines=59–62|page=42|passage=Begin vvith ''Senſe'', of ev'ry Art the Soul, / Parts anſw'ring parts ſhall '''ſlide''' into a VVhole, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|IX||All night no ruder air perplex<br>⁠Thy '''sliding''' keel, till Phosphor, bright<br>⁠As our pure love, thro’ early light<br>Shall glimmer on the dewy decks.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|finance}} To [[decrease]] in amount or value.
#: {{syn|en|slip}}
#: {{ux|en|The stock market '''slid''' yesterday after major stocks released weak quarterly results.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} To smoothly pass from one [[note]] to another by bending the [[pitch]] upwards or downwards.
# {{lb|en|regional}} To ride down [[snowy]] hills upon a [[toboggan]] or similar object for [[recreation]].
#: {{syn|en|toboggan|sled}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Ruth Fielding at Snow Camp, Or, Lost in the Backwoods|author=Alice B. Emerson|year=1913
|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=X4ZIAwAAQBAJ&pg=PT85
|text=Tom and his mates discussed some plan for a few minutes and then Tom sang out: "Who'll go '''sliding'''? There's a big bob-sled in the barn and we fixed it up yesterday morning.{{...}}"
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Corner House Girls Snowbound|author=Grace Brooks Hill|year=1919 <!-- nb: google books year metadata is 2020, but first published 1919 -->
|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-u6WOVtqqRoC&pg=PT223
|text="They're awful mean not to have taken us '''slidin'''' with them," declared Sammy, sitting on the front step and making no effort to continue the work of snow man building. "I love to '''slide'''," repeated Dot, sadly.
}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To [[go]]; to move from one place or to another.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Paolo Hewitt|title=Heaven's Promise|page=12
|passage="Gotta '''slide''', this is my stop [on the train]."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Virdez Evans|title=Actions with Consequences|publisher=iUniverse|isbn=9781663210920
|passage="Baby what are you doing why are you putting your clothes back on?" "Somebody robbd my nigga I gotta go!" I tell her. With a saddened face, she says, "What do you mean you gotta go, is he okay?" "I don't know I just know I gotta '''slide''', he's pulling up out here any min."}}
# {{lb|en|soccer}} To kick so that the ball slides along the ground with little or no turning.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 2 2021|author=Phil McNulty|title=Hungary 0-4 England|work=BBC|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58338123|passage=England captain {{w|Harry Kane}} missed a great chance to give them the lead shortly after the break but it did not prove costly as {{w|Raheem Sterling}} crowned a smooth move involving {{w|Declan Rice}}, {{w|Jack Grealish}} and {{w|Mason Mount}} to '''slide''' home his 16th goal in his past 24 international appearance after 55 minutes.}}

